>
<h1>Wire Mesh Belt Conveyors</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">A wire mesh belt conveyor uses an open-weave metal belt made from interlocking wire spirals or welded wire panels to transport products through ovens, freezers, washdown zones, and drying tunnels. The open mesh allows airflow, drainage, and radiant heat to pass directly through the belt surface, something no solid belt can do. Wire mesh belts run in everything from spiral freezers to heat-treatment furnaces, with high-nickel alloys rated to about 2,100°F, making them a standard conveyor technology for thermal processing in <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">food</a>, metalworking, electronics, and glass manufacturing.</p>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/wire-mesh-wirebelt-flat-flex-breaded.webp" width="1000" height="805" alt="Breaded chicken pieces spread across an open stainless steel wire belt running between stainless side frames." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>Breaded product on a Wire Belt Flat-Flex belt: the open wire structure lets air, coating, and liquid pass through the belt. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://wirebelt.com/products/conveyor-belts/flat-flex-conveyor-applications/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Wire Belt Company of America</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<h2 id="weave-heading">Wire Mesh Weave Types</h2>
<h3>Balanced Weave</h3>
<p>Balanced weave belts use alternating right-hand and left-hand wire spirals connected by straight cross rods (also called connector rods or crimp rods). The spirals interlock, and the cross rods hold them together. This creates a flat, flexible mesh with uniform openings. <a href="https://furnacebeltco.com/PDFCatalog.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The Furnace Belt Company</a> calls balanced weave its most preferred construction for almost any application, citing high tensile strength and good tracking. Its balanced weave mesh tables list wire diameters from about 0.035 in. to 0.225 in. Balanced weave handles light to moderate loads in baking ovens, cooling tunnels, tempering lines, and drying applications.</p>
<h3>Compound Balanced Weave</h3>
<p>Compound balanced weave nests alternating left- and right-hand spirals closely and fastens them with straight cross rods, creating a denser mesh with smaller openings. The Furnace Belt Company describes it as ideal for very small parts such as nuts, bolts, screws, and nails. Compound balanced belts also suit bakery products (cookies, crackers, pastries), electronic components, and small castings that would fall through standard balanced weave. The denser weave also distributes load more evenly, reducing product marking (belt pattern imprints) on soft goods like bread and confections.</p>
<h3>Chain-Driven Mesh</h3>
<p>Chain-driven mesh belts use roller chains on both belt edges to drive a welded or woven wire mesh panel. The chains engage sprockets at the drive and tail shafts, providing positive drive without the belt slip that can occur with friction-driven balanced weave belts. Chain-driven mesh handles the heaviest loads (castings, forgings, stampings) and highest temperatures (heat-treatment furnaces, sintering ovens).</p>
<h3>Flat Wire / Honeycomb</h3>
<p>Flat wire belts use flat strips (instead of round wire) woven into a honeycomb or herringbone pattern. Flat wire provides more surface contact area than round wire, reducing product marking on soft goods. The flat surface also supports small items more reliably. Flat wire belts are common in chocolate enrobing, bread cooling, and snack food production. Ashworth lists its <a href="https://www.ashworth.com/flat-wire-conveyor-belts/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">flat wire belts</a> for freezers, coolers, cookers, fryers, proofers, and packaging, with 1 x 1, 1/2 x 1, and 1/2 x 1/2 in. openings and 66 to 68 percent open area. Wire Belt Company states its <a href="https://wirebelt.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/FLAT-FLEX-SALES-GUIDE-SLS-008-REV2.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Flat-Flex belts</a> offer up to 86 percent open area, with 302 stainless as the standard material.</p>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/wire-mesh-ashworth-flat-wire.webp" width="1000" height="563" alt="Close view of a stainless steel flat wire belt, with flat strips bent into rectangular cells and joined by round cross rods." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>An Ashworth flat wire belt: flat strips formed into open cells and linked by cross rods. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://www.ashworth.com/flat-wire-conveyor-belts/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Ashworth</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<h3>Woven Wire Cloth</h3>
<p>Woven wire cloth belts use very fine wire woven into tight mesh patterns with the smallest openings of any wire belt type. These belts serve filtration, drying of fine powders, sintering of metal powders, and applications requiring very fine particle support. Wire cloth belts operate at lower tensions and speeds than spiral-weave belts but provide the finest available mesh opening size.</p>
