<p>Temperatures are maximum operating temperatures from The Furnace Belt Company wire selection tables. Ratings change by atmosphere: some alloys are not recommended in sulfur, lead, or zinc atmospheres. Allowable belt tension also falls steeply with temperature, and FBC notes that 304 stainless is subject to embrittlement between 500 and 1,500°F. Cost rises with alloy nickel and chromium content, wire gauge, mesh density, belt width, and edge treatment. See <a href="/conveyor-types/stainless-steel-conveyors/">stainless steel conveyor guide</a> for alloy selection details.</p>
<h2 id="apps-heading">Wire Mesh Belt Applications</h2>
<h3>Oven &amp; Baking Conveyors</h3>
<p>Wire mesh belts are the standard oven conveyor in commercial bakeries, snack food plants, and prepared food facilities. The open mesh allows hot air to circulate around all product surfaces, producing uniform browning and cooking. Baking applications typically use stainless balanced weave or flat wire belts on lines producing bread, cookies, crackers, pizza, and snack foods. Oven temperature sits well below the stainless limits above, so belt choice is driven more by product support, marking, and cleaning.</p>
<h3>Freezer &amp; <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/cooling/">Cooling Conveyors</a></h3>
<p>Spiral freezers and IQF (individually quick frozen) tunnels use stainless wire mesh belts to carry products through sub-zero environments. The mesh allows cold air to contact the product underside, accelerating freeze times. Spiral freezers stack a single continuous mesh belt in a helical tower, maximizing freezing capacity in minimal floor space.</p>
<h3>Washdown &amp; Aqueous Cleaning</h3>
<p>Wire mesh belts carry parts through aqueous wash systems, rinse tanks, and drying tunnels. Water drains through the mesh, eliminating pooling. Parts washers in automotive, aerospace, and electronics manufacturing rely on wire mesh belts to move components through multi-stage wash, rinse, and dry cycles. The mesh openings allow spray nozzles positioned below the belt to reach the product underside.</p>
<h3>Heat Treatment &amp; Sintering</h3>
<p>Industrial furnaces use chain-driven mesh belts for continuous heat treatment of metal parts. Applications include hardening, tempering, annealing, brazing, and sintering of powdered metal components. Belt temperatures can exceed 2,000°F in controlled-atmosphere furnaces. FBC lists 314 stainless for copper brazing and powder metal sintering, and rates Inconel 601 and 80/20 nickel-chrome to 2,100°F.</p>
<h3>Coating &amp; Enrobing</h3>
<p>Chocolate enrobing, glazing, and coating lines use fine-mesh wire belts. Product rides on the belt through a curtain of liquid coating. Excess coating drains through the mesh. Air knives or vibration removes additional excess. The mesh pattern should be fine enough that the product does not deform into the openings but open enough for drainage. Food-grade <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">sanitary belt</a> standards apply to enrobing applications.</p>
<h2 id="engineering-heading">Specifying Wire Mesh Belts</h2>
<h3>Mesh Opening Size</h3>
<p>The mesh opening determines the smallest product that can ride on the belt without falling through. For food baking, opening size is set by the smallest product and the marking it can tolerate. For parts washing, openings must be smaller than the smallest part. For heat treatment, openings should be large enough for atmosphere circulation but small enough to support the lightest part. Mesh opening is specified as the clear distance between adjacent wires, not wire center-to-center.</p>
<h3>Belt Width &amp; Edge Treatment</h3>
<p>Wire mesh belts come in a wide range of widths. Ashworth lists flat wire belts from 4.5 to 208 in. wide. Edge treatment affects belt tracking and product containment. Welded edges seal the outside wire to prevent snagging and product loss. Chain edges use roller chains for positive drive and tracking. Clinched edges fold the outer wire back on itself for a smooth perimeter. The edge type must match the drive method (friction vs. sprocket) and the application's product containment needs.</p>
<h3>Belt Tracking &amp; Support</h3>
<p>Wire mesh belts track on rails, slider bars, or roller supports. At high temperatures, belt elongation from thermal expansion requires spring-loaded or gravity take-up systems to maintain tension. Belt guides or flanged sprockets prevent lateral drift. Proper support spacing prevents belt sag between supports, which causes product shifting and uneven cooking in oven applications. Ask the belt supplier for support spacing based on belt type, load, and temperature.</p>
<h3>Belt Life &amp; Replacement</h3>
<p>Wire mesh belt life depends on temperature, load, speed, and belt material. Life is shortest in high-temperature furnaces, where creep and oxidation work on the wire, and longest in moderate-temperature food lines with good tension control. FBC recommends stress relieving a new furnace belt by raising temperature in steps of 300°F over several belt cycles. Belt failure typically manifests as wire fatigue breaks at cross rod connections, edge chain elongation, or mesh distortion from creep at sustained high temperatures.</p>
<h2 id="maint-heading">Cleaning &amp; Maintenance</h2>
<p><strong>Daily cleaning (food applications):</strong> CIP spray bars above and below the belt apply hot water, detergent, and sanitizer while the belt runs at low speed. Bottom sprays are critical because debris lodges on the return side. Effective CIP requires full spray coverage across the belt width. Set pressure and chemistry per the belt and sanitation chemical supplier.</p>
<p><strong>Weekly deep cleaning:</strong> Release belt tension, run the belt through an alkaline soak, and brush stubborn deposits from cross rod junctions. Automated belt washers use rotating brushes that scrub both belt surfaces as the belt runs through the washer housing.</p>
<p><strong>Lubrication:</strong> Most wire mesh belts in food and high-temperature applications run dry (no lubrication). In ambient industrial applications, food-grade lubricants can extend edge chain life on chain-driven mesh. Never use petroleum-based lubricants on food-contact belts.</p>
<p><strong>Tension monitoring:</strong> Wire mesh belts stretch over time. Excess slack causes tracking problems, sag between supports, and erratic belt speed. Check tension weekly. Spring-loaded take-ups compensate automatically. Screw take-ups require manual adjustment. Replace the belt or remove a section when it has stretched beyond the take-up adjustment range. Damaged sections can be spliced: Wire Belt Company sells pre-formed joining strands and clips for joins and minor repairs.</p>
